    "explanation": "Both parties are told the same thing, and the arithmetic is the same either way.\n\nThe route deviation is real. The GPS trace (EV-GPS-02813) measures 12.42 km between the recorded pickup and dropoff, against a booked optimal of 9.2 km on the trip record — an excess of 3.22 km, or 35.00%. That clears both POL-RD-01 establishment tests (above 20% and above 0.8 km). The trace is clean for policy purposes: 98 fixes at a 10 s interval, 100% coverage, no gaps and max gap 0 s, so POL-RD-07 does not impair it and no independent corroboration is required. The deviation is established but not material — 35.00% is below the 50% materiality figure and 3.22 km is below the 8.0 km absolute figure — so the POL-RD-06 driver-profile check is not triggered by materiality, and in any event the driver's deviation_flags_90d is 0, below both the 3-or-more and the 0.05-ratio limbs, so no repeat-pattern referral arises. The lifetime counter prior_deviation_flags is context only under POL-GEN-01 and grounds nothing.\n\nThe money, however, is not a distance refund. This is an UPFRONT_QUOTE trip (quoted fare SGD 21.50, charged total SGD 28.30), so under POL-RD-11 POL-RD-02 does not run and no distance-or-time refund is available however large the excess; the detour cost falls on the driver. The rider's remedy is confined to POL-RD-11(a): the amount by which the charge exceeds the quote, less the pass-through and statutory lines properly chargeable under POL-RD-09. Those lines are nil here — no ERP gantries, no tolls, airport surcharge SGD 0.00 on a trip that neither originates nor ends at an airport, waiting-time fee SGD 0.00. The refund is therefore 28.30 − 21.50 − 0.00 = SGD 6.80, which is already at a 0.05 increment. The rider's framing of the SGD 6.80 as a distance refund reaches the right figure by the wrong route; the amount is granted, but the contested SGD 7.50 exceeds it and the remaining SGD 0.70 is not recoverable on these facts.\n\nThe driver's statement that 